# What a creature IS, on top of how it walks.
#
# Core's behaviors are about locomotion and deliberately nothing else. A record says a body holds its
# tile, ambles, closes on what it notices, opens the gap, keeps a distance, or tidies litter — six ways
# of moving, none of which mentions fighting. The original's five said why instead, and four of them
# meant something about combat.
#
# This file is the translation. Nothing here is new engine surface; it is the original's vocabulary
# written out of the parts Core does offer.
#
#   Attack on sight        Pursue, and swing on contact                       — Rules.OnContact
#   Attack when attacked   Wander, and chase whoever lands a hit              — Beasts.Answer
#   Friendly               Wander, with no rule pointing it at anybody
#   Stationary             Stationary
#   Guard                  Wander, carrying the guard key, roused by a crime  — Beasts.CallGuards
#   Caster                 Shadow, and loose a bolt from the gap it keeps     — Beasts.Volley
#
# ⚠ A guard is marked on the CREATURE RECORD, in the attributes an editor hangs on the template. That
# is where a per-species fact belongs: every copy of that creature is a guard, and a body that became
# one at spawn would be a guard by accident.

shared model Beasts

    # Carried by a creature that answers a crime. Any number above zero means yes.
    public string Guard = "guard";

    # How far a shout carries, in tiles. The original asked each guard for its own noticing range;
    # one number is the same rule with the authoring taken out of it.
    public integer GuardRange = 10;

    # How many answer. A whole map converging on one body reads as a bug rather than as law.
    public integer GuardsWhoAnswer = 3;

    # ── What a fresh body is made of ──────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # A creature arrives carrying a copy of its record and nothing else: the engine has never heard of
    # health, so the pool is written on here from the number its author wrote. The ceiling goes on with
    # it, because the CLIENT draws a bar from the pair and has no way to run a formula.
    #
    # ⚠ The authored number lives under the same key the pool does, so a body starts with what the
    # record says and has it overwritten here rather than read out of somewhere else.

    public function Wake(Npc it)
        integer full = it.Number(Vitals.Health);

        if full < 1
            yield;
        end if

        if Combat.AfterDark()
            full = Math.Max(1, Math.Round(full * Combat.NightHealth));
        end if

        it.SetNumber(Vitals.MaxHealth, full);
        it.SetNumber(Vitals.Health, full);
    end function

    # ── Night falling, and lifting ────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # Everything already standing is re-scaled at the boundary, IN PROPORTION: a creature at half
    # health is at half health afterwards, and its bar does not jump. Without this a body that has been
    # alive since the afternoon is the only thing in the world that night does not touch.
    #
    # ⚠ Which hour it was is kept as a world value rather than in a field, because it has to survive
    # a restart. A server that came up at midnight and read nothing would scale everything a second
    # time at dawn.

    public string Hour = "beastshour";

    public function Turn()
        string now = World.TimeOfDay();
        string was = World.Text(Beasts.Hour);

        if now == was
            yield;
        end if

        World.SetText(Beasts.Hour, now);

        boolean before = was == Combat.Night;
        boolean after = now == Combat.Night;

        # Dusk to night is a boundary. Dusk to dawn, somehow, is not.
        if before == after
            yield;
        end if

        real ratio = Combat.NightHealth;

        if not after
            ratio = 1.0 / Combat.NightHealth;
        end if

        loop for map = 1 to World.Records("Maps")
            loop each body in World.NpcsOn(map)
                Beasts.Scale(body, ratio);
            end loop
        end loop
    end function

    function Scale(Npc it, real ratio)
        integer ceiling = it.Number(Vitals.MaxHealth);

        if ceiling < 1
            yield;
        end if

        integer full = Math.Max(1, Math.Round(ceiling * ratio));
        integer left = Math.Max(1, Math.Round(it.Number(Vitals.Health) * ratio));

        if left > full
            left = full;
        end if

        it.SetNumber(Vitals.MaxHealth, full);
        it.SetNumber(Vitals.Health, left);
    end function

    # ── Fighting back ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    # Every creature answers a hit, whatever its record says about noticing.
    #
    # For one authored to close on what it sees this changes nothing; it was already coming. For one
    # authored to amble this is "attacks when attacked" entire — it never notices anybody, and nothing
    # else ever points it at somebody. For one authored to open the gap it runs, as a body that flees
    # should when it is hit.
    public function Answer(Npc it, Player who)
        # Whether it turned is dropped on purpose. A handle whose body has already left cannot be
        # pointed anywhere, and the creature that was hit being gone is the outcome the hitter wanted.
        it.Chase(who);
    end function

    # And the same between two creatures, so a bite from behind turns the bitten one around rather
    # than leaving it walking away from a fight it is losing.
    public function AnswerNpc(Npc it, Npc attacker)
        it.ChaseNpc(attacker);
    end function

    # ── Bodies that fight from a gap ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # 🔴 A body that keeps its distance arrives ONCE and then stands there. Contact is raised on the beat
    # it settles, which is the first bolt — and after that nothing happens to it again, because nothing
    # has: it is holding station exactly as it was authored to. So the volley is driven from the world’s
    # own beat instead, and the engine’s own cooldown paces it.
    #
    # ⚠ Whatever a body is after is read off the ENGINE rather than remembered here. It is the engine
    # that decided who it noticed and the engine that will drop them; a copy kept in this file would be
    # aiming at somebody who walked off the map two beats ago.

    # What Core calls the behavior that closes to a distance and keeps it.
    public string KeepsDistance = "shadow";

    public function Volley()
        loop for map = 1 to World.Records("Maps")
            loop each body in World.NpcsOn(map)
                if body.Behavior == Beasts.KeepsDistance
                    Beasts.LooseFrom(body);
                end if
            end loop
        end loop
    end function

    function LooseFrom(Npc it)
        Player? at = it.Target;

        if at.HasValue()
            Fight.Loose(it, at.Value());
            yield;
        end if

        Npc? other = it.TargetNpc;

        if other.HasValue()
            Fight.LooseAt(it, other.Value());
        end if
    end function

    # ── Guards ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    # Somebody did something the town will not have. The guards standing near it come after them.
    #
    # ⚠ World.NpcsNear answers nearest first, so taking the first few takes the ones who could
    # plausibly have seen it.
    public function CallGuards(Player on, integer map, integer x, integer y)
        Npc[] nearby = World.NpcsNear(map, x, y, Beasts.GuardRange);
        integer sent = 0;

        loop each body in nearby
            if sent >= Beasts.GuardsWhoAnswer
                yield;
            end if

            if body.Number(Beasts.Guard) > 0
                if body.Chase(on)
                    sent = sent + 1;
                end if
            end if
        end loop

        if sent > 0
            on.Message("The guard has seen that.");
        end if
    end function

end model
